## 1. Introduction

The task-based approach to automation models the economy at the level of tasks rather than jobs. A job is a bundle of tasks; AI alters the cost, productivity, and feasibility of the tasks that compose it. This architecture separates three distinct effects: automation (displacement of labor from tasks), augmentation (productivity gains for labor in tasks), and reinstatement (creation of new labor-intensive tasks). The central theoretical question is whether these forces, operating through the labor market, generate bounded reallocation or structural technological unemployment.

This chapter provides a rigorous reformulation of the task-based macroeconomic architecture of transformative AI. It corrects several internal inconsistencies in earlier formulations, derives the aggregate labor share from explicit task-level primitives, proves a corrected bounded-reallocation theorem under stated assumptions, characterizes the breakdown of the safety valve under embodied automation, and provides a welfare analysis of the demand-side externality generated by automation under heterogeneous marginal propensities to consume and sticky wages.

The analysis proceeds as follows. Section 2 establishes the formal environment with consistent notation distinguishing productivity from task masses. Section 3 derives task-level production and cost functions. Section 4 aggregates to the economy-wide labor share and proves the dual-capital labor share proposition. Section 5 models the innovation margin between automation and reinstatement R&D. Section 6 derives labor demand and the reallocation equation from primitives. Section 7 proves the bounded-reallocation theorem under explicit assumptions. Section 8 characterizes the breakdown of the safety valve when both cognitive and physical automation are active. Section 9 analyzes demand-side feedback and proves constrained inefficiency of the decentralized equilibrium. Section 10 models capital ownership and fiscal capacity. Section 11 describes the computational strategy and provides a baseline calibration. Section 12 concludes.

