## 2. The Formal Environment

### 2.1 Notation and Aggregation Assumptions

The economy consists of a continuum of tasks indexed by $i \in \mathcal{I} = [0,1]$. Each task is characterized by three attributes:

- cognitive intensity $\kappa_i \in [0,1]$;
- embodiment index $\rho_i \in [0,1]$;
- interpersonal intensity $\iota_i \in [0,1]$.

Occupations are partitioned into a triad $o \in \{C, P, I\}$, where $C$ denotes cognitive tasks $(\kappa_i \approx 1, \rho_i \approx 0)$, $P$ denotes physical tasks $(\rho_i \to 1)$, and $I$ denotes interpersonal and affective tasks $(\iota_i \to 1)$. A job is a bundle of tasks $J_o = \{i \in \mathcal{I} : \theta_{o,i} = 1\}$, where $\theta_{o,i} \in \{0,1\}$ indicates whether task $i$ is required for occupation $o$.

Labor supply is fixed in the short run:
$$L_t = L_{C,t} + L_{P,t} + L_{I,t} = \bar{L}. \tag{1}$$

Production utilizes two distinct forms of artificial capital: cognitive AI capital $K_{C,t}$ with return $r_{C,t}$ and productivity $A_{C,t}$; and embodied robotic capital $K_{P,t}$ with return $r_{P,t}$ and productivity $A_{P,t}$. Labor-augmenting productivity is $A_{L,t}$.

**Notation convention.** Throughout, $A_{j,t}$ denotes productivity, while $\mathcal{A}_{j,t}$ denotes task mass. This distinction is maintained rigorously. The task masses are:
$$\mathcal{A}_{C,t} = m_{C,t} d_{C,t}, \qquad \mathcal{A}_{P,t} = m_{P,t} d_{P,t}, \tag{2}$$
where $m_{j,t} \in [0,1]$ is the measure of tasks in class $j$ exposed to automation, and $d_{j,t} \in [0,1]$ is the depth of automation. The mass of newly created labor-intensive tasks is $n_t$. The labor-task share is:
$$N_t = 1 - \mathcal{A}_{C,t} - \mathcal{A}_{P,t} + n_t. \tag{3}$$

**Elasticities.** The maintained assumptions are: task-level elasticities of substitution between labor and capital $\sigma_C, \sigma_P \in (0,1)$; elasticity of substitution across tasks $\varepsilon > 0$; reduced-form aggregate elasticity $\sigma \equiv \sigma_C = \sigma_P$ unless otherwise stated.

**Aggregation assumptions.** We assume:

(A1) Within the automated set $\{i : \alpha_{j,i} \to 1\}$, all tasks share the same automation intensity and factor prices.

(A2) Within the non-automated set $\{i : \alpha_{j,i} = 0\}$, all tasks share the same labor-augmenting productivity and factor prices.

(A3) The CES aggregator over tasks is symmetric, so that the aggregate price index depends only on the relative masses $N_t$ and $\mathcal{A}_{j,t}$ and productivities $A_{j,t}$.

Under (A1)–(A3), the aggregate labor share admits the closed form derived in Section 4.

### 2.2 Task-Level Production with Dual Capital

For any cognitive task $i \in C$, output is produced with labor and cognitive AI capital:
$$y_t(i) = \left[ (1-\alpha_{C,i}) (A_{L,t} l_t(i))^{\frac{\sigma_C-1}{\sigma_C}} + \alpha_{C,i} (A_{C,t} k_{C,t}(i))^{\frac{\sigma_C-1}{\sigma_C}} \right]^{\frac{\sigma_C}{\sigma_C-1}}. \tag{4}$$

For any physical task $i \in P$, output is produced with labor and robotic capital:
$$y_t(i) = \left[ (1-\alpha_{P,i}) (A_{L,t} l_t(i))^{\frac{\sigma_P-1}{\sigma_P}} + \alpha_{P,i} (A_{P,t} k_{P,t}(i))^{\frac{\sigma_P-1}{\sigma_P}} \right]^{\frac{\sigma_P}{\sigma_P-1}}, \tag{5}$$
with the embodiment mapping
$$\alpha_{P,i} = \Phi(\rho_i, \tau_{\text{robotics}}) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp[-\phi_0 - \phi_1 \rho_i - \phi_2 \tau_{\text{robotics}}]}, \qquad \phi_1, \phi_2 > 0. \tag{6}$$

For interpersonal tasks $i \in I$, AI augments labor without substituting for it:
$$y_t(i) = A_{L,t} l_t(i). \tag{7}$$

### 2.3 Task-Level Cost Minimization

Cost minimization for task $i \in \{C, P\}$ yields the unit cost:
$$c_t(i) = \left[ (1-\alpha_{j,i})^{\sigma_j} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_j} + \alpha_{j,i}^{\sigma_j} \left(\frac{r_{j,t}}{A_{j,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_j} \right]^{\frac{1}{1-\sigma_j}}, \qquad j \in \{C, P\}. \tag{8}$$

The labor cost share in task $i$ is:
$$s_{L,t}(i) = \frac{(1-\alpha_{j,i})^{\sigma_j} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_j}}{(1-\alpha_{j,i})^{\sigma_j} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_j} + \alpha_{j,i}^{\sigma_j} \left(\frac{r_{j,t}}{A_{j,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_j}}, \qquad j \in \{C, P\}. \tag{9}$$

For interpersonal tasks, $s_{L,t}(i) = 1$.

Define the relative factor price terms:
$$X_t = \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma}, \qquad Z_{j,t} = \left(\frac{r_{j,t}}{A_{j,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma}, \qquad j \in \{C, P\}. \tag{10}$$

For a non-automated task $(\alpha_{j,i} = 0)$, the labor cost share simplifies to:
$$s_{L,t}^{\text{non-auto}} = \frac{X_t}{X_t + Z_{j,t}}. \tag{11}$$

For a fully automated task $(\alpha_{j,i} \to 1)$, $s_{L,t}^{\text{auto}} = 0$.

### 2.4 Aggregate Output and the Price Index

Aggregate output is a CES aggregate over tasks:
$$Y_t = \left[ \int_0^1 y_t(i)^{\frac{\varepsilon-1}{\varepsilon}} \, di \right]^{\frac{\varepsilon}{\varepsilon-1}}, \qquad \varepsilon > 0. \tag{12}$$

Under (A1)–(A3) and symmetric task aggregation, the aggregate price index is:
$$P_t = \left[ N_t c_{\text{non-auto},t}^{1-\varepsilon} + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} c_{C,\text{auto},t}^{1-\varepsilon} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} c_{P,\text{auto},t}^{1-\varepsilon} \right]^{\frac{1}{1-\varepsilon}}, \tag{13}$$
where $c_{\text{non-auto},t}$ is the unit cost of a representative non-automated task, and $c_{j,\text{auto},t}$ is the unit cost of a fully automated task of type $j$.

