## 3. Aggregation and the Labor Share

### 3.1 Derivation of the Aggregate Labor Share

The aggregate labor share is:
$$s_{L,t} = \frac{w_t L_t}{P_t Y_t}. \tag{14}$$

Under cost minimization, the labor cost share in each task equals the elasticity of the unit cost with respect to the wage. Aggregating over tasks using the CES price index:
$$s_{L,t} = \frac{N_t X_t}{N_t X_t + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}}. \tag{15}$$

This is the reduced-form labor share. It follows from the task-level cost shares under (A1)–(A3) and the symmetry of the CES aggregator.

**Proposition 1 (Closed-Form Labor Share with Dual Capital).** Under (A1)–(A3), symmetric task aggregation, and binary automation, the aggregate labor share of income is:
$$s_{L,t} = \frac{N_t X_t}{N_t X_t + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}}, \tag{16}$$
where
$$N_t = 1 - \mathcal{A}_{C,t} - \mathcal{A}_{P,t} + n_t, \qquad \mathcal{A}_{j,t} = m_{j,t} d_{j,t}, \tag{17}$$
$$X_t = \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma}, \qquad Z_{j,t} = \left(\frac{r_{j,t}}{A_{j,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma}, \qquad j \in \{C, P\}. \tag{18}$$

**Proof.** Let $\mathcal{N}_t = N_t X_t$ and $\mathcal{A}_t = \mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}$. Under (A1)–(A3), the aggregate labor share equals the ratio of labor-weighted to total factor-weighted task masses. The labor-weighted task mass is $\mathcal{N}_t$, and the capital-weighted task mass is $\mathcal{A}_t$. Hence:
$$s_{L,t} = \frac{\mathcal{N}_t}{\mathcal{N}_t + \mathcal{A}_t}. \tag{19}$$
Substituting the definitions yields (16). $\square$

**Remark.** When $\mathcal{A}_{P,t} = 0$, the formula collapses to the single-capital expression. When $\mathcal{A}_{C,t} = \mathcal{A}_{P,t} = 0$, $s_{L,t} = 1$.

### 3.2 Log-Linearization

Taking logs of (16) and differentiating around a baseline:
$$\hat{s}_{L,t} = (1 - s_{L,t}) \left[ \widehat{N_t} - \omega_{C,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}} - \omega_{P,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{P,t}} + (1-\sigma)(\hat{w}_t - \hat{A}_{L,t}) - \omega_{C,t} \hat{Z}_{C,t} - \omega_{P,t} \hat{Z}_{P,t} \right], \tag{20}$$
where the capital-share weights are:
$$\omega_{j,t} = \frac{\mathcal{A}_{j,t} Z_{j,t}}{\mathcal{A}_t}, \qquad \omega_{C,t} + \omega_{P,t} = 1. \tag{21}$$

Using $\hat{Z}_{j,t} = (1-\sigma)(\hat{r}_{j,t} - \hat{A}_{j,t})$:
$$\hat{s}_{L,t} = (1 - s_{L,t}) \left[ \widehat{N_t} - \omega_{C,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}} - \omega_{P,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{P,t}} + (1-\sigma) \left( \hat{w}_t - \hat{A}_{L,t} - \omega_{C,t} \hat{r}_{C,t} - \omega_{P,t} \hat{r}_{P,t} + \omega_{C,t} \hat{A}_{C,t} + \omega_{P,t} \hat{A}_{P,t} \right) \right]. \tag{22}$$

**Interpretation.** Automation in either cognitive or physical tasks exerts downward pressure on the labor share through the terms $-\omega_{j,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{j,t}}$. Reinstatement raises $N_t$ and offsets this pressure. The relative magnitudes of $\omega_{C,t}$ and $\omega_{P,t}$ determine which automation channel dominates.

