## 4. The Innovation Margin: Automation vs. Reinstatement

### 4.1 Directed Technical Change

A continuum of innovators of mass one chooses research effort between automation R&D (which raises $\mathcal{A}_{j,t}$) and reinstatement R&D (which creates new labor-intensive tasks $n_t$). Let $z_t \in [0,1]$ be the share of research effort allocated to reinstatement. Profits from each activity are proportional to the size of the relevant task set and to the factor-price advantage:
$$\Pi_{N,t} = \pi_N z_t \left(\frac{w_t}{r_t}\right)^{\eta}, \qquad \Pi_{A,t} = \pi_A (1 - z_t) \left(\frac{r_t}{w_t}\right)^{\eta}, \qquad \eta > 0, \tag{23}$$
where $r_t$ is a weighted average of $r_{C,t}$ and $r_{P,t}$:
$$r_t = \frac{\mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t}}{\mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}} r_{C,t} + \frac{\mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}}{\mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}} r_{P,t}. \tag{24}$$

### 4.2 Equilibrium Allocation

Innovators equate expected profits:
$$\pi_N \left(\frac{w_t}{r_t}\right)^{\eta} = \pi_A \left(\frac{r_t}{w_t}\right)^{\eta}. \tag{25}$$

Solving:
$$z_t^* = \frac{1}{1 + \frac{\pi_A}{\pi_N} \left(\frac{r_t}{w_t}\right)^{2\eta}}. \tag{26}$$

**Reinstatement.** The mass of new labor-intensive tasks is:
$$n_t = \bar{n} + \nu z_t^* \left(\frac{w_t}{r_t}\right)^{\eta} = \bar{n} + \frac{\nu \left(\frac{w_t}{r_t}\right)^{\eta}}{1 + \frac{\pi_A}{\pi_N} \left(\frac{r_t}{w_t}\right)^{2\eta}}, \qquad \nu > 0. \tag{27}$$

This specification is bounded in $[\bar{n}, \bar{n} + \nu]$ and respects the comparative statics of directed technical change.

**Remark.** This block is explicitly a reduced-form representation of the innovation margin. It is not a fully dynamic model with forward-looking innovators, entry, uncertainty, or spillovers. It should be interpreted as a static approximation that captures the direction of innovative response to relative factor prices.

### 4.3 Net Displacement

Define net displacement as:
$$D_t^{\text{net}} = \mathcal{A}_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} - n_t. \tag{28}$$

The stabilizing force of reinstatement is microfounded: as automation displaces labor and pushes $w_t / r_t$ down, $z_t^*$ rises and $n_t$ increases.

