## 5. Labor Demand and the Reallocation Equation

### 5.1 Task-Level Labor Demand

Cost minimization for a non-automated cognitive task $i$ implies:
$$l_t(i) = \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{-\sigma} (1-\alpha_{C,i})^{\sigma} \left(\frac{c_t(i)}{P_t}\right)^{-\varepsilon} \frac{Y_t}{P_t}. \tag{29}$$

Under (A2), all non-automated cognitive tasks share the same labor-augmenting productivity and factor prices. Aggregating over the mass $1 - \mathcal{A}_{C,t}$ of such tasks:
$$L_{C,t} = (1 - \mathcal{A}_{C,t}) \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{-\sigma} \frac{Y_t}{P_t} \cdot \left(\frac{c_{\text{non-auto},t}}{P_t}\right)^{-\varepsilon}. \tag{30}$$

This is the correct aggregation. Labor demand in non-automated cognitive tasks is **proportional** to the mass of such tasks, not inversely proportional.

### 5.2 Log-Linearization of Labor Demand

Taking logs and differentiating:
$$\hat{L}_{C,t} = \hat{Y}_t - \sigma (\hat{w}_t - \hat{A}_{L,t}) - \frac{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}}{1 - \mathcal{A}_{C,t}} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}} + \varepsilon (\hat{P}_t - \hat{c}_{\text{non-auto},t}). \tag{31}$$

Under the maintained assumption that the price index moves proportionally with the unit cost of non-automated tasks, the last term vanishes. Define the automation share:
$$\varsigma_{C,t} = \frac{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}}{1 - \mathcal{A}_{C,t}}. \tag{32}$$

Then:
$$\hat{L}_{C,t} = \hat{Y}_t - \sigma \hat{w}_t + \sigma \hat{A}_{L,t} - \varsigma_{C,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}}. \tag{33}$$

### 5.3 The Reallocation Equation

Fixed labor supply implies $\Delta L_{I,t} = -\Delta L_{C,t}$. Using (33):
$$\Delta L_{I,t} = L_{C,t-1} \left[ \varsigma_{C,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}} + \sigma \hat{w}_t - \sigma \hat{A}_{L,t} - \hat{Y}_t \right]. \tag{34}$$

Under the maintained wage–output co-movement $\hat{w}_t \approx \hat{Y}_t$, which holds when labor's share is roughly stable at the margin:
$$\Delta L_{I,t} = L_{C,t-1} \left[ \varsigma_{C,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}} - \sigma \hat{A}_{L,t} + (\sigma - 1) \hat{Y}_t \right]. \tag{35}$$

This is the corrected reallocation equation. The two forces driving interpersonal employment are:

1. **Net displacement:** $\varsigma_{C,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}} - \widehat{n_t}$.
2. **Labor released by augmentation:** $(1-\sigma) \hat{A}_{L,t}$.

**Comparative statics.**
$$\frac{\partial \Delta L_{I,t}}{\partial \mathcal{A}_{C,t}} > 0, \qquad \frac{\partial \Delta L_{I,t}}{\partial n_t} < 0, \qquad \frac{\partial \Delta L_{I,t}}{\partial A_{L,t}} < 0, \qquad \frac{\partial \Delta L_{I,t}}{\partial \sigma} < 0. \tag{36}$$

If $\sigma = 0.5$, half of the time saved by AI is immediately reinvested into doing more of the same task. Only half is released to the broader economy.

