## 7. Embodied AI and the Breakdown of the Safety Valve

### 7.1 Dual-Capital Production with Physical Automation

When humanoid robotics and autonomous physical infrastructure reach economic viability, the physical sector ceases to function as a safe harbor for displaced cognitive workers. The task-level production function for physical tasks is given by (5), with $\alpha_{P,i} = \Phi(\rho_i, \tau_{\text{robotics}})$.

The unit cost is:
$$c_t(i) = \left[ (1-\alpha_{P,i})^{\sigma_P} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_P} + \alpha_{P,i}^{\sigma_P} \left(\frac{r_{P,t}}{A_{P,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_P} \right]^{\frac{1}{1-\sigma_P}}. \tag{42}$$

### 7.2 Multidimensional Net Displacement

The net displacement vector becomes:
$$\mathbf{D}_t^{\text{net}} = \begin{bmatrix} \mathcal{A}_{C,t} - n_{C,t} \\ \mathcal{A}_{P,t} - n_{P,t} \end{bmatrix}. \tag{43}$$

Labor market clearing requires:
$$L_t = L_{C,t} + L_{P,t} + L_{I,t} = \bar{L}. \tag{44}$$

When both cognitive and physical automation expand simultaneously $(\mathcal{A}_{C,t} > 0$ and $\mathcal{A}_{P,t} > 0)$, the traditional non-cognitive safety valve disappears if physical tasks are also automated.

### 7.3 Proposition 3 (Breakdown of the Safety Valve)

**Proposition 3.** Assume:

1. Labor supply is fixed: $L_{C,t} + L_{P,t} + L_{I,t} = \bar{L}$.
2. Cognitive automation is active: $\mathcal{A}_{C,t} > 0$.
3. Physical automation is active: $\mathcal{A}_{P,t} > 0$.
4. Displaced cognitive and physical workers are absorbed only by the interpersonal sector $I$.
5. The interpersonal sector has bounded absorption capacity $\kappa_I \bar{L}$, where:
$$\kappa_I = \kappa_0 \left(\frac{w_{I,t}}{w_t}\right)^{\epsilon_I}, \qquad \kappa_0 \in (0,1), \ \epsilon_I > 0. \tag{45}$$

Define displaced labor as:
$$D_{C,t} = -\Delta L_{C,t} > 0, \qquad D_{P,t} = -\Delta L_{P,t} > 0. \tag{46}$$

Then:

**(i)** If $D_{C,t} + D_{P,t} \leq \kappa_I \bar{L}$, displaced labor is fully absorbed by the interpersonal sector and no structural unemployment emerges.

**(ii)** If $D_{C,t} + D_{P,t} > \kappa_I \bar{L}$, structural technological unemployment emerges:
$$U_t^* = D_{C,t} + D_{P,t} - \kappa_I \bar{L} > 0. \tag{47}$$

**(iii)** The aggregate labor share converges to the share of all remaining labor tasks:
$$s_{L,t} \to s_{L,I} + s_{L,n} = \frac{w_t L_{I,t}}{Y_t} + \frac{w_t L_{n,t}}{Y_t} \quad \text{as} \quad \mathcal{A}_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} \to 1, \tag{48}$$
where $L_{n,t}$ is employment in newly created tasks. This limit is strictly positive as long as $L_{I,t} + L_{n,t} > 0$. Only if all remaining tasks are also automated does $s_{L,t} \to 0$.

**Proof.** Parts (i) and (ii) follow directly from labor market clearing and the definition of $\kappa_I \bar{L}$. For part (iii), from Proposition 1:
$$s_{L,t} = \frac{N_t X_t}{N_t X_t + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}}. \tag{49}$$
As $\mathcal{A}_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} \to 1$, we have $N_t \to n_t + (1 - \mathcal{A}_{C,t} - \mathcal{A}_{P,t})$. The only tasks remaining are interpersonal tasks and newly created tasks. Since interpersonal tasks are produced with $y_t(i) = A_{L,t} l_t(i)$, their labor cost share is one. Newly created tasks are labor-intensive by construction. Hence:
$$s_{L,t} \to \frac{w_t (L_{I,t} + L_{n,t})}{Y_t} > 0, \tag{50}$$
as long as $L_{I,t} + L_{n,t} > 0$. $\square$

**Remark.** The correct limit is the share of all remaining labor tasks, not zero. Embodied AI removes the physical fallback, but the interpersonal and reinstatement fallbacks may remain. The question is whether $I$ and new tasks have enough absorption capacity and demand.

### 7.4 Macroeconomic Implications

The aggregate labor share faces downward pressure from both cognitive software and physical robotics:
$$s_{L,t} = \frac{N_t X_t}{N_t X_t + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}} \to s_{L,I} + s_{L,n} \quad \text{as} \quad (\mathcal{A}_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t}) \to 1. \tag{51}$$

Capital returns diverge:
$$\max \left( \frac{\dot{r}_{C,t}}{r_{C,t}}, \frac{\dot{r}_{P,t}}{r_{P,t}} \right) \gg \frac{\dot{w}_t}{w_t}. \tag{52}$$

Transitional unemployment becomes more severe because physical and cognitive skills both undergo structural obsolescence.

