## 9. Capital Ownership and Fiscal Capacity

### 9.1 Ownership Shares as Policy Levers

Let $\theta_W + \theta_K + \theta_G = 1$ denote the shares of AI capital owned by workers, private capitalists, and the government. Effective worker income is:
$$Y_{W,t} = s_{L,t} Y_t + \theta_W (1 - s_{L,t}) Y_t. \tag{62}$$

The effective labor income share is:
$$s_{L,t}^{\text{eff}} = s_{L,t} + \theta_W (1 - s_{L,t}). \tag{63}$$

If $\theta_W$ is small, a falling $s_{L,t}$ translates almost one-for-one into distributional divergence.

### 9.2 Fiscal Base and Sustainability

Let taxes be levied on labor income, consumption, and capital income:
$$T_t = \tau_L w_t L_t + \tau_C C_t + \tau_K r_t K_t. \tag{64}$$

Since $w_t L_t = s_{L,t} Y_t$ and $r_t K_t = (1 - s_{L,t}) Y_t$:
$$T_t = \tau_L s_{L,t} Y_t + \tau_C C_t + \tau_K (1 - s_{L,t}) Y_t. \tag{65}$$

As $s_{L,t} \to s_{L,I} + s_{L,n}$:
$$T_t \to \tau_L (s_{L,I} + s_{L,n}) Y_t + \tau_C C_t + \tau_K (1 - s_{L,I} - s_{L,n}) Y_t. \tag{66}$$

If $\tau_K$ cannot rise, the fiscal gap widens. This is the AI analogue of the petrostate problem.

### 9.3 Ownership Dynamics

In a fully dynamic setting, $\theta_W$ can rise endogenously if workers hold claims on AI capital:
$$\theta_{W,t+1} = \theta_{W,t} + \zeta s_{L,t} Y_t, \qquad \zeta > 0, \tag{67}$$
where $\zeta$ is the rate at which wage income is converted into capital claims. The scenario generator treats $(\theta_W, \zeta, \tau_K)$ as policy levers.

