## Appendix A: Task-Level Cost Minimization and Factor Demands

### A.1 The Cost Minimization Problem

Consider a task of type $j \in \{C, P\}$ with automation intensity $\alpha_{j,i} \in [0,1]$. The production function is:
$$y_t(i) = \left[ (1-\alpha_{j,i}) (A_{L,t} l_t(i))^{\frac{\sigma_j-1}{\sigma_j}} + \alpha_{j,i} (A_{j,t} k_{j,t}(i))^{\frac{\sigma_j-1}{\sigma_j}} \right]^{\frac{\sigma_j}{\sigma_j-1}}. \tag{A.1}$$

The firm minimizes cost $w_t l_t(i) + r_{j,t} k_{j,t}(i)$ subject to producing one unit of output. The Lagrangian is:
$$\mathcal{L} = w_t l_t(i) + r_{j,t} k_{j,t}(i) + \lambda \left[ 1 - y_t(i) \right]. \tag{A.2}$$

### A.2 First-Order Conditions

The first-order conditions with respect to $l_t(i)$ and $k_{j,t}(i)$ are:
$$w_t = \lambda \frac{\partial y_t(i)}{\partial l_t(i)}, \qquad r_{j,t} = \lambda \frac{\partial y_t(i)}{\partial k_{j,t}(i)}. \tag{A.3}$$

Compute the marginal products. Let:
$$\Upsilon_t(i) = (1-\alpha_{j,i}) (A_{L,t} l_t(i))^{\frac{\sigma_j-1}{\sigma_j}} + \alpha_{j,i} (A_{j,t} k_{j,t}(i))^{\frac{\sigma_j-1}{\sigma_j}}. \tag{A.4}$$

Then $y_t(i) = \Upsilon_t(i)^{\frac{\sigma_j}{\sigma_j-1}}$. The marginal product of labor is:
$$\frac{\partial y_t(i)}{\partial l_t(i)} = \frac{\sigma_j}{\sigma_j-1} \Upsilon_t(i)^{\frac{1}{\sigma_j-1}} (1-\alpha_{j,i}) \frac{\sigma_j-1}{\sigma_j} A_{L,t}^{\frac{\sigma_j-1}{\sigma_j}} l_t(i)^{-\frac{1}{\sigma_j}}. \tag{A.5}$$

Simplifying:
$$\frac{\partial y_t(i)}{\partial l_t(i)} = (1-\alpha_{j,i}) A_{L,t}^{\frac{\sigma_j-1}{\sigma_j}} \Upsilon_t(i)^{\frac{1}{\sigma_j-1}} l_t(i)^{-\frac{1}{\sigma_j}}. \tag{A.6}$$

Similarly:
$$\frac{\partial y_t(i)}{\partial k_{j,t}(i)} = \alpha_{j,i} A_{j,t}^{\frac{\sigma_j-1}{\sigma_j}} \Upsilon_t(i)^{\frac{1}{\sigma_j-1}} k_{j,t}(i)^{-\frac{1}{\sigma_j}}. \tag{A.7}$$

### A.3 Factor Demand Ratios

Dividing the two first-order conditions:
$$\frac{w_t}{r_{j,t}} = \frac{1-\alpha_{j,i}}{\alpha_{j,i}} \left(\frac{A_{L,t}}{A_{j,t}}\right)^{\frac{\sigma_j-1}{\sigma_j}} \left(\frac{k_{j,t}(i)}{l_t(i)}\right)^{\frac{1}{\sigma_j}}. \tag{A.8}$$

Rearranging:
$$\frac{k_{j,t}(i)}{l_t(i)} = \left(\frac{\alpha_{j,i}}{1-\alpha_{j,i}}\right)^{\sigma_j} \left(\frac{A_{L,t}}{A_{j,t}}\right)^{\sigma_j-1} \left(\frac{w_t}{r_{j,t}}\right)^{\sigma_j}. \tag{A.9}$$

### A.4 Unit Cost Function

Substitute the factor demand ratio into the production function and solve for the cost of producing one unit. The unit cost is:
$$c_t(i) = \left[ (1-\alpha_{j,i})^{\sigma_j} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_j} + \alpha_{j,i}^{\sigma_j} \left(\frac{r_{j,t}}{A_{j,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_j} \right]^{\frac{1}{1-\sigma_j}}. \tag{A.10}$$

**Verification.** When $\sigma_j \to 1$, the CES production function becomes Cobb–Douglas:
$$y_t(i) = (A_{L,t} l_t(i))^{1-\alpha_{j,i}} (A_{j,t} k_{j,t}(i))^{\alpha_{j,i}}. \tag{A.11}$$

The unit cost becomes:
$$c_t(i) = \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\alpha_{j,i}} \left(\frac{r_{j,t}}{A_{j,t}}\right)^{\alpha_{j,i}} \cdot \frac{1}{(1-\alpha_{j,i})^{1-\alpha_{j,i}} \alpha_{j,i}^{\alpha_{j,i}}}. \tag{A.12}$$

Taking the limit of (A.10) as $\sigma_j \to 1$ and applying L'Hôpital's rule confirms this expression.

### A.5 Labor Cost Share

The labor cost share in task $i$ is:
$$s_{L,t}(i) = \frac{w_t l_t(i)}{c_t(i) y_t(i)}. \tag{A.13}$$

Using Shephard's lemma, $\partial c_t(i)/\partial w_t = l_t(i)/y_t(i)$. Hence:
$$s_{L,t}(i) = \frac{w_t}{c_t(i)} \frac{\partial c_t(i)}{\partial w_t}. \tag{A.14}$$

Computing the derivative:
$$\frac{\partial c_t(i)}{\partial w_t} = \frac{1}{1-\sigma_j} c_t(i)^{\sigma_j} (1-\sigma_j) (1-\alpha_{j,i})^{\sigma_j} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{-\sigma_j} \frac{1}{A_{L,t}}. \tag{A.15}$$

Simplifying:
$$\frac{\partial c_t(i)}{\partial w_t} = c_t(i)^{\sigma_j} (1-\alpha_{j,i})^{\sigma_j} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{-\sigma_j} \frac{1}{A_{L,t}}. \tag{A.16}$$

Therefore:
$$s_{L,t}(i) = \frac{(1-\alpha_{j,i})^{\sigma_j} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_j}}{(1-\alpha_{j,i})^{\sigma_j} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_j} + \alpha_{j,i}^{\sigma_j} \left(\frac{r_{j,t}}{A_{j,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_j}}. \tag{A.17}$$

This confirms equation (9) in the main text.

### A.6 Limiting Cases

For a non-automated task $(\alpha_{j,i} = 0)$:
$$s_{L,t}^{\text{non-auto}} = \frac{\left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_j}}{\left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_j} + 0} = 1. \tag{A.18}$$

For a fully automated task $(\alpha_{j,i} \to 1)$:
$$s_{L,t}^{\text{auto}} = \frac{0}{0 + \left(\frac{r_{j,t}}{A_{j,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma_j}} = 0. \tag{A.19}$$

For an interior task $(\alpha_{j,i} \in (0,1))$, the labor cost share is strictly between 0 and 1.

