## Appendix B: Derivation of the Aggregate Labor Share (Proposition 1)

### B.1 The CES Aggregator

Aggregate output is:
$$Y_t = \left[ \int_0^1 y_t(i)^{\frac{\varepsilon-1}{\varepsilon}} \, di \right]^{\frac{\varepsilon}{\varepsilon-1}}, \qquad \varepsilon > 0. \tag{B.1}$$

The aggregate price index $P_t$ is defined implicitly by $P_t Y_t = \int_0^1 p_t(i) y_t(i) \, di$, where $p_t(i)$ is the price of task $i$. Under monopolistic competition with flexible prices, $p_t(i) = \frac{\varepsilon}{\varepsilon-1} c_t(i)$. The price index satisfies:
$$P_t = \left[ \int_0^1 p_t(i)^{1-\varepsilon} \, di \right]^{\frac{1}{1-\varepsilon}}. \tag{B.2}$$

Under (A1)–(A3), the economy partitions into three sets:

- Non-automated tasks: mass $N_t$, unit cost $c_{\text{non-auto},t}$.
- Automated cognitive tasks: mass $\mathcal{A}_{C,t}$, unit cost $c_{C,\text{auto},t}$.
- Automated physical tasks: mass $\mathcal{A}_{P,t}$, unit cost $c_{P,\text{auto},t}$.

The price index becomes:
$$P_t = \left[ N_t c_{\text{non-auto},t}^{1-\varepsilon} + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} c_{C,\text{auto},t}^{1-\varepsilon} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} c_{P,\text{auto},t}^{1-\varepsilon} \right]^{\frac{1}{1-\varepsilon}}. \tag{B.3}$$

### B.2 Aggregate Labor Demand

The demand for task $i$ is:
$$y_t(i) = \left(\frac{p_t(i)}{P_t}\right)^{-\varepsilon} \frac{Y_t}{P_t}. \tag{B.4}$$

For a non-automated task, labor input is:
$$l_t(i) = \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{-\sigma} \left(\frac{c_{\text{non-auto},t}}{P_t}\right)^{-\varepsilon} \frac{Y_t}{P_t}. \tag{B.5}$$

Aggregating over the mass $N_t$ of non-automated tasks:
$$L_t = N_t \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{-\sigma} \left(\frac{c_{\text{non-auto},t}}{P_t}\right)^{-\varepsilon} \frac{Y_t}{P_t}. \tag{B.6}$$

### B.3 Aggregate Labor Share

The aggregate labor share is:
$$s_{L,t} = \frac{w_t L_t}{P_t Y_t}. \tag{B.7}$$

Substituting (B.6):
$$s_{L,t} = N_t \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma} \left(\frac{c_{\text{non-auto},t}}{P_t}\right)^{-\varepsilon}. \tag{B.8}$$

Under (A3), the relative price term satisfies:
$$\left(\frac{c_{\text{non-auto},t}}{P_t}\right)^{-\varepsilon} = \frac{X_t}{X_t + \frac{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}}{N_t} Z_{C,t} + \frac{\mathcal{A}_{P,t}}{N_t} Z_{P,t}} \cdot \frac{1}{1 - s_{L,t}}. \tag{B.9}$$

A more direct derivation uses the cost-share aggregation. Since the aggregate cost function is homothetic, the aggregate labor share equals the weighted average of task-level labor cost shares, with weights equal to task expenditure shares. Under (A1)–(A3):
$$s_{L,t} = \frac{N_t X_t}{N_t X_t + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}}, \tag{B.10}$$
where
$$X_t = \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma}, \qquad Z_{j,t} = \left(\frac{r_{j,t}}{A_{j,t}}\right)^{1-\sigma}. \tag{B.11}$$

**Proof of (B.10).** Let $\mathcal{N}_t = N_t X_t$ and $\mathcal{A}_t = \mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}$. The aggregate cost function is:
$$\mathcal{C}_t = Y_t \left[ N_t c_{\text{non-auto},t}^{1-\varepsilon} + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} c_{C,\text{auto},t}^{1-\varepsilon} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} c_{P,\text{auto},t}^{1-\varepsilon} \right]^{\frac{1}{1-\varepsilon}}. \tag{B.12}$$

By Shephard's lemma:
$$L_t = \frac{\partial \mathcal{C}_t}{\partial w_t}, \qquad K_{j,t} = \frac{\partial \mathcal{C}_t}{\partial r_{j,t}}. \tag{B.13}$$

The labor share is:
$$s_{L,t} = \frac{w_t}{C_t} \frac{\partial C_t}{\partial w_t}. \tag{B.14}$$

Computing the derivative:
$$\frac{\partial C_t}{\partial w_t} = Y_t \frac{1}{1-\varepsilon} \left[ \cdot \right]^{\frac{1}{1-\varepsilon}-1} \left[ N_t (1-\varepsilon) c_{\text{non-auto},t}^{-\varepsilon} \frac{\partial c_{\text{non-auto},t}}{\partial w_t} \right]. \tag{B.15}$$

Using $\frac{\partial c_{\text{non-auto},t}}{\partial w_t} = \frac{c_{\text{non-auto},t}^{\sigma}}{A_{L,t}} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{-\sigma}$ and simplifying:
$$\frac{\partial C_t}{\partial w_t} = Y_t P_t^{\varepsilon} N_t c_{\text{non-auto},t}^{-\varepsilon} \frac{c_{\text{non-auto},t}^{\sigma}}{A_{L,t}} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{-\sigma}. \tag{B.16}$$

Under (A3), $c_{\text{non-auto},t}^{\sigma-1} = X_t + Z_{j,t}$ for the relevant $j$. After simplification:
$$s_{L,t} = \frac{N_t X_t}{N_t X_t + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}}. \tag{B.17}$$

$\square$

### B.4 Log-Linearization

Take logs of (B.17):
$$\ln s_{L,t} = \ln \mathcal{N}_t - \ln(\mathcal{N}_t + \mathcal{A}_t). \tag{B.18}$$

Differentiate:
$$\hat{s}_{L,t} = \widehat{\mathcal{N}_t} - \frac{\mathcal{N}_t}{\mathcal{N}_t + \mathcal{A}_t} \widehat{\mathcal{N}_t} - \frac{\mathcal{A}_t}{\mathcal{N}_t + \mathcal{A}_t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_t}. \tag{B.19}$$

Since $\mathcal{N}_t/(\mathcal{N}_t + \mathcal{A}_t) = s_{L,t}$ and $\mathcal{A}_t/(\mathcal{N}_t + \mathcal{A}_t) = 1 - s_{L,t}$:
$$\hat{s}_{L,t} = (1 - s_{L,t}) \left[ \widehat{\mathcal{N}_t} - \widehat{\mathcal{A}_t} \right]. \tag{B.20}$$

Now:
$$\widehat{\mathcal{N}_t} = \widehat{N_t} + \hat{X}_t = \widehat{N_t} + (1-\sigma)(\hat{w}_t - \hat{A}_{L,t}), \tag{B.21}$$
$$\widehat{\mathcal{A}_t} = \omega_{C,t} \left(\widehat{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}} + \hat{Z}_{C,t}\right) + \omega_{P,t} \left(\widehat{\mathcal{A}_{P,t}} + \hat{Z}_{P,t}\right), \tag{B.22}$$
where
$$\omega_{j,t} = \frac{\mathcal{A}_{j,t} Z_{j,t}}{\mathcal{A}_t}, \qquad \omega_{C,t} + \omega_{P,t} = 1. \tag{B.23}$$

Substituting:
$$\hat{s}_{L,t} = (1 - s_{L,t}) \left[ \widehat{N_t} - \omega_{C,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}} - \omega_{P,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{P,t}} + (1-\sigma)(\hat{w}_t - \hat{A}_{L,t}) - \omega_{C,t} \hat{Z}_{C,t} - \omega_{P,t} \hat{Z}_{P,t} \right]. \tag{B.24}$$

Using $\hat{Z}_{j,t} = (1-\sigma)(\hat{r}_{j,t} - \hat{A}_{j,t})$:
$$\hat{s}_{L,t} = (1 - s_{L,t}) \left[ \widehat{N_t} - \omega_{C,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}} - \omega_{P,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{P,t}} + (1-\sigma) \left( \hat{w}_t - \hat{A}_{L,t} - \omega_{C,t} \hat{r}_{C,t} - \omega_{P,t} \hat{r}_{P,t} + \omega_{C,t} \hat{A}_{C,t} + \omega_{P,t} \hat{A}_{P,t} \right) \right]. \tag{B.25}$$

This confirms equation (22) in the main text.

